id,title,total,days,first,last,corrective,shape,guard
編號,標題,總次數,發生天數,首次,最近,矯正措施,形狀,守衛
1,"Stopped part-way through an agreed queue and reported instead of continuing",3,2,2026-09-08,2026-09-09,"Scripts/queue_heartbeat.zsh plus a /loop that reads it: the next item is named by a file, not by memory","A unit of work finishes, the report is written, and the turn ends -- with items still on the list the user had asked to be worked through. Nothing fails; the report is accurate about what was done and silent about what was not started","Scripts/queue_heartbeat.zsh (prints the next unchecked item, or IDLE when the switch is off)"
2,A new source file is not picked up by an incremental build,1,1,2026-09-10,2026-09-10,Delete .build/release.yaml before the first build after adding a file; touch Package.swift does NOT work because SwiftPM hashes manifest content,"A file is added to a target directory, the compile phase is completely clean, and the LINKER reports undefined symbol: <the new function>. That reads as a missing library or a bad declaration, not as a file that was never compiled. release.yaml was three hours stale and still listed the old source set","git diff --stat after a regenerate, and: ls -l .build/release.yaml -- if it predates the new file, the plan cannot know about it"
3,Added a way to change the target window and left the identity half undone,3,2,2026-09-09,2026-09-10,"When adding an action that RETARGETS anything, treat currentWindowIdentity() as a required second half, not an optional extra: grep every Synthesiser for the override before calling the action done. Three platforms, three spellings, one silent failure","focus succeeds, the replay reports every action completed, and every later coordinate still resolves against the window measured before the replay began. Nothing errors -- each coordinate is a real number, just relative to the wrong window","grep -c currentWindowIdentity across all Synthesiser files; a synthesiser with 0 returns the protocol default of 0, and 0 is exactly what replay reads as cannot-tell"
4,Ran a tool against a filename that does not exist and read no output as a clean result,3,1,2026-09-10,2026-09-10,"Any conclusion whose CONTENT is a zero needs a positive control run by the SAME command -- something known to match. For a path, put ls -l on the expected OUTPUT in the same command; on the third occurrence that is what contradicted the zero within a second","A tool prints nothing and the nothing looks exactly like good news: objdump on libgtk-4-1.dll (real name gtk-4-1.dll) read as zero symbols; grep -cE for errors after zsh compile.sh (real name compile.zsh) read as a clean build; and compile.zsh -winui, a flag that does not exist, printed Missing source file which is not a compiler diagnostic and so scored 0 errors too","ls -l every path before handing it to a tool AND ls -l the expected output after; before writing down a zero, name what would make that command print non-zero, then make it print once"
5,"Read a dead process as a slow one, and invented a mechanism to explain it",1,1,2026-09-10,2026-09-10,"Before explaining WHY something is slow, check that it is still running: pgrep the process, or diff against the last version that worked (git stash / git checkout -- the file). An explanation must come after the process is confirmed alive, never before.","P52 gained a fourth arm; four per-arm arrays were still count:3, so lastSentinel[3] terminated the process before step one. The log ended after CONFIG with no crash message and no non-zero exit anywhere I looked, which reads exactly like a benchmark that has stalled. I attributed it to AppKit throttling redraws in a background window -- a real mechanism, which is what made it convincing -- and re-ran it in the foreground to test that. Reverting the file to HEAD took one command and answered it: the original completed, so I had broken it.","git checkout -- <file> and re-run BEFORE theorising, whenever the thing that changed is mine. It is one command and it separates my defect from the platform."
6,"Reported a test suite as passing from a grep of its output, without its exit code",2,2,2026-09-10,2026-09-16,"Read the runner verdict, not a filter of the log. run_checked.zsh exists for exactly this and prints an exit status; `grep -aE ... | tail` does not. When a summary line is wanted AND the verdict, capture the run to a file, echo $? on its own line, then grep the file.","A rename removed LazyVGrid.resolve and plan.columnWidths, which GridLayoutTests still used, so SwiftCrossUITests could not BUILD. Scripts/test.sh runs more than one test invocation; a later one built and printed `Test run with 68 tests ... passed`, and my grep for `Test run with|X` matched that line and nothing else. The compile error had the word `error:` and no checkmark, so it fell outside the pattern. I committed with the message `Scripts/test.sh passes`, which was false, and only found it an hour later when the same filter finally showed the error on an unrelated run. SECOND OCCURRENCE 2026-09-16, a new disguise: a background build run as `compile.zsh P23 2>&1 | tail -6`. stdout is block-buffered through a pipe and stderr is not, so the last six lines were warnings; `Done.` and the one `error:` were both cut off, and the exit code belonged to tail. I read `errors: 0` and said so. What contradicted it was not the log at all -- the ARTEFACT was still two hours old (entry 9's corrective), and re-running with the full log to a file gave rc=1.",Never let the pattern decide the verdict. Run through run_checked.zsh and read its exit status on its own line before writing any claim about a build or a test run. On this tree that means never piping a build through tail/grep in the same command that judges it: redirect the whole log to a file and read rc separately.
7,Reported progress as done when it only compiled or was only answered,1,1,2026-09-11,2026-09-11,"A progress table must not have a done column. It needs three -- answered / compiles / ran-and-measured -- each demanding different evidence: a line number, a BUILD-RC=0 WITH the output timestamp, and a number with a control. A row whose third column is empty is not finished however full the first two are","Six rows, five marked done in bold. Every sentence individually true -- the cells do compile, the question was answered, the split was agreed -- but placed in a column headed status, which reads as is-this-finished. #127 compiled and originInWindow had never been called; #122 and #109 were an answer and a nod with zero lines of code. Recorded because I spent that same day catching the identical shape in files arriving marked NOT COMPILED HERE","Before reporting an item ask: can I paste a number? If not the words are compiles-not-run, and results.csv2 is where the number belongs"
8,Compared a measurement against a constant that was never measured,1,1,2026-09-11,2026-09-11,"A comparison has two sides. Before writing that X is larger than Y and therefore something is wrong, ask of Y what you asked of X: where did this number come from? Be most suspicious of the constants everyone knows -- 60 Hz, 96 DPI, 1000 vs 1024, the default timeout -- because those are exactly the ones nobody looks up","CompositionTarget.Rendering measured at 140.4 Hz was declared a defect because the display is 60 Hz. Two numbers in that sentence, one measured and one from memory; dxdiag /t reports 144Hz, so 141.6 Hz is the refresh rate less a few dropped frames and the event always fired once per frame. Worse than a private detour: the wrong premise was handed to the other machine, which derived a sound diagnosis from it and wrote a fix using RenderingEventArgs.renderingTime -- a type swift-winui does not bind at all. Not entry 4: the measured number was non-zero and correct, so no positive control on the measured side would ever find it","Record the command that produces Y next to Y. testapp/FAQ.md now writes dxdiag /t <file> beside the 144 Hz, and notes that wmic returns nothing on Windows 11 26200"
9,"Measured the previous build, because building and packaging are different steps",1,1,2026-09-15,2026-09-15,"Before any measurement or screenshot on a platform whose artifact is PACKAGED (Android APK, iOS .app, a bundle), verify the artifact's own timestamp, not the build's exit code. `ls -l` the .apk in the same command that installs it, and read the app's own self-report on screen rather than inferring from behaviour.","compile.zsh -android builds into .build/ while the APK is packaged from a separate swift-bundler build in .build-bundler/. The compile returned 0, adb install printed Success, the app started and rendered -- and every screenshot was of the previous build. P57 reported `lazy rows: NO` and logcat showed the eager path, so I concluded the LazyListRows conformance was invisible at runtime on Android and spent an hour on linker and conformance-record theories. The Swift had been correct from the first build; the device had never been given it.",Make the app state the fact under test on screen (P57 prints `lazy rows: yes/NO`) AND add a control the same way (`control -- scrolling lists:`) -- a control that also reads NO would have pointed at the binary within seconds instead of at the feature. Then check the artifact's mtime before believing either.
10,Verified on the one platform where the defect was impossible,1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"When a change is CONDITIONAL on a platform, the build that exercises the condition cannot see what the change does to the builds that do not. Name the platforms where the flag is OFF and run one of them -- for this tree that is `Scripts/test.sh` on the host, which is also the cheapest.","Adding ""AndroidBackend"" to Package.swift's migratedToSwift6 list. The target exists only when SCUI_ANDROID=1, so on every other build the manifest's own typo guard fired `migratedToSwift6 names 'AndroidBackend', which is not a target in this package` and the package would not load at all. Verified repeatedly and happily on Android -- five rebuilds, an APK, a device run, launch flags checked in logcat -- because Android is exactly where the name IS a target and the fatal error cannot occur. macOS, iOS, Linux and Windows were all broken at that moment. Caught by Scripts/test.sh minutes later, not by anything in the Android loop.","Before editing a manifest or any other file read by every platform, ask which platforms take the OTHER branch, then run the cheapest one before claiming the change works. A conditional edit verified only under its own condition is unverified."
11,"Put test logs in the wrong directory, where .gitignore guaranteed nobody would report it",1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"Before running a test app by hand, set SCUI_DEBUG_EVENTS_DIR to an EMPTY directory: SCUI_DEBUG_EVENTS_DIR=$(mktemp -d) ./testapp/output/Pn --debug ... . Empty is the load-bearing half -- it makes 'this run wrote no log' distinguishable from 'last run's log is still there'.","Nine p*-debug-events.log files in the REPOSITORY ROOT, noticed by a human reading a directory listing rather than by any tool. They are what a test app writes when run by hand: the SCUI_DEBUG_EVENTS_DIR fallback is the current directory, and test.zsh sets it while running the binary directly does not. .gitignore covers the pattern, so git status was clean, git ls-files found zero, and every commit, push and test run passed. Worse than litter: coverage-matrix.csv2 already recorded for P34 that a stale log reads exactly like a fresh pass, and today a root copy of p70-debug-events.log sat beside the one in testapp/debug-events that I read.","What .gitignore hides, no check will look at for you. Ignored is not harmless, it is only quiet -- so the discipline has to be at the point of running, not at the point of review."
12,"Asked the queue whose turn it was, never asked origin what already existed",1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"Before starting a queue item, ask ORIGIN what exists rather than the queue whose turn it is: git fetch && git log origin/develop --oneline -S""<the API name>"" | head. -S searches content, not messages, because the other side may never name the symbol in a subject line. On a shared branch, fetching is part of starting a TASK, not part of starting a session.","#121 was implemented twice. Git reported NO conflict because the two implementations were in different files; the compiler reported it as 'invalid redeclaration of keyboardShortcut(_:modifiers:)' after both were finished, and mine was reverted in full. Windows pushed theirs at 09:56 and I began writing mine at about 10:30 -- 35 minutes of warning, with my only fetch at 09:30 when origin really was 0 behind. queue.md said the item was 'blocked on Mac', which was true when written; they then unblocked themselves with a route that needed nothing from this side.","A queue file records what someone believed when they wrote it, not what holds now - an intention, not a lock, and one that never expires reads exactly like a current fact. Version control has no concept of a feature being built twice, only of the same lines changing, so no check after the work can catch this."
13,"Read a stale README, then set out to rebuild something that already existed and had been used",1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"Before building any infrastructure, ask the filesystem and git rather than the prose: ls the directory the document calls empty, git log -- the path the feature would live at, grep the scripts for its name. Three commands, seconds. And when a document disagrees with the tree, fix the document first, or the next person walks into it believing they checked.","testapp/actions/README.md:23 says 'ios/ empty; planned'. I concluded iOS could not be driven, wrote three CGEvent drivers at Simulator and then DeviceHub, and after they failed made 'build an XCUITest target' the first priority -- writing into the queue that this was the real reason actions/ios is empty. It holds 58 files. The XCUITest runner exists at testapp/iosContainer/xcodeTestRunner/Tests/ActionFileUITests.swift, is wired into test_ios.zsh --actionfile, and had already driven real taps (commit 7f4193dc). I only checked because the user said they remembered doing it. The real gap is one case that THROWS unsupported for keydown/keyup/key.","Entry 12 with a different document: that one asked the queue whose turn it was instead of asking origin what existed. A stale document reads exactly like an accurate one, and nothing reports that a README disagrees with the directory it describes."
14,Read half the evidence and took a transition for the verdict,1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"In a sequence of state transitions the verdict is the LAST value, not the first. Before calling a log line a defect: find the code that emits it (onChange prints transitions), grep for later lines with the same name and read the final value, prefer the verdict the app renders on screen, and finish reading your own instrument -- both ends of the distribution, not the big one.","P70 logged DISABLED FOCUS reported true, then false, in the same second. I read the first line, declared that a disabled control had taken focus, and wrote a fix on two backends. The four lines were the complete trace of CORRECT behaviour: the framework does if !backend.focus(widget) { binding.wrappedValue = false }, so the app sets the binding optimistically and the backend refusal writes it back. My own instrumentation had already printed 2 took=false against 51 took=true and the two falses were exactly the two that should be refused - I stopped reading at the 51. The app also RENDERS the verdict, disabled button reports focused: no (correct), legible in two screenshots I had already taken.",State in the action file header WHERE the verdict is read from. These files already say what each step asserts; this adds which line answers it.
15,"An unquoted comma in a hand-written CSV, and the file was rejected in silence",1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"Scripts/check_action_file_fields.sh, wired into Scripts/test.sh after check_action_files.sh. It reads every testapp/actions/*/*.csv WITHOUT asking git -- which is the gap: the Swift suite's check is 'every tracked action file parses', and a file written five minutes ago is not tracked yet. Proved in both directions before being trusted, and its first version was too strict (took platform as the last column, wrong for files with a trailing target) and false-positived on P60-open-settings.csv.","The harness exited 0, the app launched and was screenshotted, the summary printed RENDER COMPLETE, and my grep of the log for actionfile|replay|error found nothing -- so I concluded the file had never been replayed. The rejection was one line in testapp/output/p23-actionfile.log: 'failed: line 48: unknown platform not restart'. A comma inside an unquoted note field shifted every field after it one place left. The grep missed it because the harness greps only TEST_SUMMARY_PATTERN (RENDER COMPLETE|rows|selection|table|cell|header) and P23-column-sorting.csv contains none of those words; the same harness had shown me that line earlier the same day for P23-row-selection.csv, because that NAME happens to contain 'selection'.","Same defect as the global CSV rule, from the other direction: that rule is about splitting a CSV on commas when READING one; this is writing one, where an unquoted comma is just as silent. And a filter written to make output readable decides which failures are visible."
16,"Used `path` as a loop variable, which in zsh IS PATH",1,1,2026-09-16,2026-09-16,"Never name a zsh variable path (nor status, options, argv, cdpath, manpath, fpath, watch). And when command not found appears in a script that worked a moment ago, print $PATH BEFORE replacing the command: the first fix swapped mktemp for a named directory, touched nothing, and made the next failure wear a different name.","A sweep script worked for a three-app trial and failed the full run with command not found: mktemp, then command not found: date after that dependency was replaced. Both read as missing coreutils on this machine; a standalone probe found every one of them present. The cause was for path in $output_dir/P*.exe -- zsh ties path to PATH as an array, so the loop replaced the search path with an .exe file and every external command afterwards was gone. Exit code 127 names the command, not the assignment, and the symptom was twelve lines below the cause. The trial passed because naming apps explicitly takes the other branch and never enters the glob loop: a short run that works and a long run that does not, with nothing changed between them.","grep -rn for path in across the scripts after any fix, and read $PATH at the first command-not-found rather than swapping the command"
